The statement accurately reports the findings of a real 2016 ESMT Berlin study (Rocholl & Stahmer). It is therefore factually correct as a description of that paper, though the underlying allocation of funds is open to differing economic interpretations.

The study examines the €215.9 billion disbursed under the first two Greek programs (2010–mid-2015). Its breakdown is:
- €9.7 billion (<5 %) financed the Greek state budget (i.e., primary deficits and other direct fiscal needs);
- €86.9 billion repaid maturing debt;
- €52.3 billion paid interest;
- the €139.2 billion subtotal for debt service is indeed just over 64 % of the total;
- the remainder went mainly to bank recapitalization (€37.3 billion) and smaller items (ECB/IMF fees, etc.).

These figures follow directly from the official disbursement and use-of-proceeds data published by the European Commission, EFSF/ESM and IMF; the ESMT authors simply re-aggregated them. Later program reviews and the third bailout (2015–2018) do not retroactively change the arithmetic for the period the study covers.

How the result should be judged depends on the question being asked:
- As a narrow accounting statement it is uncontroversial: once Greece had moved into primary surplus, the overwhelming bulk of new official loans was required simply to roll over existing debt and service interest, plus to repair the banking system.
- As a claim that “the money never reached Greece” or “only rescued foreign banks,” it is incomplete. Debt repayment extinguished liabilities that would otherwise have remained on the Greek sovereign’s balance sheet (or triggered a disorderly default); bank recapitalization preserved domestic financial stability; and the alternative of immediate hard default in 2010–12 carried its own large costs. The same data can therefore be read as evidence that the programs socialized and lengthened Greece’s debt rather than as proof that Greeks received nothing.

In short, the quoted percentages are right; the policy moral drawn from them is a matter of interpretation, not of arithmetic.

Final answer: accurate (ESMT 2016 figures correct)
